UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3: Tell a story and prepare readable notes', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Tell an original story aloud and make readable notes that help a listener follow it.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Keep the independent response and two written check responses; record oral retelling or performance where required by the task.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Create a new example using a completely different character or setting, show the same skill, then explain to another person how you checked its accuracy.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"WE DO — attempt each prompt before comparing to the feedback.\n1. Put these events in sensible order: We dried the dog; the dog jumped in a puddle; we went home.\nCOMPARE: The dog jumped in a puddle. We went home. We dried the dog. Drying follows getting wet.\n2. Improve the opening \"He ran away\" by naming the character, place and reason for moving.\nCOMPARE: One option: \"At the school oval, Ben ran towards the gate to catch his rolling ball.\" It answers who, where and why.\n3. Shorten \"Then the gardener found the missing bucket beside the tree\" into three or four note words.\nCOMPARE: \"gardener / bucket / beside tree\" preserves the important detail; full grammar is not required in notes.\n4. Read these notes aloud as a two-sentence story: rain—path / sister—umbrella. Add a pause between events.\nCOMPARE: \"Rain soaked the path. My sister opened her umbrella.\" Pause naturally after the first complete thought.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"YOU DO — complete the following substantial original task, not a copied model.\nPlan a real or invented four-event story about an unexpected visitor. Make four brief cue lines (not full sentences), tell the story in four or five complete spoken sentences, then record which cue helped most and one improvement you made after rehearsing. Write or record the final version.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Tell an original story aloud and make readable notes that help a listener follow it.","lesson_number":4,"lesson_role":"Speaking and handwriting","materials":["Paper or typing area","Pencil for optional evidence"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper to copy only the original independent prompt; have the child respond in writing or orally as required. Save a photograph or brief parent observation for the learning record.","outcome_codes":["EN2-OLC-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance: Check notes are genuinely brief, events connect, who and where are introduced, and the recorded or transcribed retelling is understandable; reward oral fluency separately from spelling. Accept plausible original solutions where the text supports them; require the learner to explain at least one independent choice.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original demonstration: I tell a short story about losing my hat: First I put it on near the oval. Next the wind carried it over the fence. Then the groundskeeper returned it. Finally I thanked her. Each event leads naturally to the next.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Do not copy whole paragraphs into note cards, because you may read rather than tell. Correction: \"gardener / bucket / beside tree\" preserves the important detail; full grammar is not required in notes.\n\nParent: Assess comprehension and language rather than penalising speech or motor difficulties unrelated to the objective. Keep written models out of the child role.","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A listener needs to know what happened first, next and at the end. Temporal words signal changes, but events must also connect logically. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A listener needs to know what happened first, next and at the end. Temporal words signal changes, but events must also connect logically.","Do not assume saying finally makes an event a logical ending.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Tell events in an order that makes sense\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"An orientation tells listeners who is involved and where events occur. Without it, later pronouns and actions are confusing. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["An orientation tells listeners who is involved and where events occur. Without it, later pronouns and actions are confusing.","A listener cannot see the picture in your head; introduce new people and places.","Copy the model word-for-word and call that independent work.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Give your listener a clear character and place\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Speaking notes should be short prompts, not every word of the speech. Key nouns and verbs help you remember without reading a script. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["Speaking notes should be short prompts, not every word of the speech. Key nouns and verbs help you remember without reading a script.","Do not copy whole paragraphs into note cards, because you may read rather than tell.","Treat a missing detail as a stated fact.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Choose the most useful notes\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A practice retell lets you check if events make sense aloud. Use pauses and an audible voice; invite a listener to say which part was unclear. The other choices either name a known misunderstanding or avoid explaining the actual strategy.","options":["A practice retell lets you check if events make sense aloud. Use pauses and an audible voice; invite a listener to say which part was unclear.","Speaking quickly does not make a story clear; rehearse meaning and pace.","Skip the prompt because the first idea sounded good.","Ignore the words and invent a more exciting event."],"question":"Which idea is taught in the stage \"Speak, listen and improve one detail\"?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The dog jumped in a puddle. We went home. We dried the dog. Drying follows getting wet. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["The dog jumped in a puddle. We went home. We dried the dog. Drying follows getting wet.","Do not assume saying finally makes an event a logical ending.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Put these events in sensible order: We dried the dog; the dog jumped in a puddle\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"One option: \"At the school oval, Ben ran towards the gate to catch his rolling ball.\" It answers who, where and why. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["One option: \"At the school oval, Ben ran towards the gate to catch his rolling ball.\" It answers who, where and why.","A listener cannot see the picture in your head; introduce new people and places.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Improve the opening \"He ran away\" by naming the character, place and reason for \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"\"gardener / bucket / beside tree\" preserves the important detail; full grammar is not required in notes. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["\"gardener / bucket / beside tree\" preserves the important detail; full grammar is not required in notes.","Do not copy whole paragraphs into note cards, because you may read rather than tell.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Shorten \"Then the gardener found the missing bucket beside the tree\" into three \", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"\"Rain soaked the path. My sister opened her umbrella.\" Pause naturally after the first complete thought. This explanation uses the original evidence or language point, rather than treating a misconception as the correct response.","options":["\"Rain soaked the path. My sister opened her umbrella.\" Pause naturally after the first complete thought.","Speaking quickly does not make a story clear; rehearse meaning and pace.","Any answer is equally supported.","There is no need to compare your attempt."],"question":"After trying \"Read these notes aloud as a two-sentence story: rain—path / sister—umbrella. Add\", which feedback is most useful?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Meaningful understanding is shown when a learner transfers the method to new text or speech and explains their evidence.","marking_guide":"Require an original relevant example, accurate terminology and an explanation grounded in the example.","question":"Use your own example to demonstrate orientation and explain the evidence supporting your choice.","sample_answer":"Original demonstration: I tell a short story about losing my hat: First I put it on near the oval. Next the wind carried it over the fence. Then the groundskeeper returned it. Finally I thanked her. Each event leads naturally to the next.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"Explaining a correction helps the learner distinguish an effective strategy from a plausible-sounding but inaccurate approach.","marking_guide":"Require a genuine relevant error, accurate correction and a reason that refers to the target concept.","question":"Identify and correct a common mistake relating to cue words in this lesson.","sample_answer":"Common error: Do not copy whole paragraphs into note cards, because you may read rather than tell. Correction: \"gardener / bucket / beside tree\" preserves the important detail; full grammar is not required in notes.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Submit your original independent work and your brief explanation of the key choices you made.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y3-w01-l4","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; compare the existing Coming Soon card and official syllabus content points before marking verified.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise orientation.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Your goal is: Tell an original story aloud and make readable notes that help a listener follow it."],"guided":["Attempt each guided prompt without looking ahead.","Compare your attempt with the supplied specific feedback.","Guided comparison: One option: \"At the school oval, Ben ran towards the gate to catch his rolling ball.\" It answers who, where and why."],"learn":["Study the first worked demonstration carefully.","Name the exact words or events that make it work.","Demonstration: I tell a short story about losing my hat: First I put it on near the oval. Next the wind carried it over the fence. Then the groundskeeper r"],"quiz":["Read the whole question and eliminate misconceptions.","Prefer the option that quotes or uses actual evidence.","Likely trap: Do not copy whole paragraphs into note cards, because you may read rather than tell."],"submit":["Include your own response and a short reason.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation before sending.","Success looks like: Tell an original story aloud and make readable notes that help a listener follow it."],"teach0":["Focus on tell events in an order that makes sense.","Hold this teaching point: A listener needs to know what happened first, next and at the end. Temporal words signal changes, but events must also c","Worked comparison: The dog jumped in a puddle. We went home. We dried the dog. Drying follows getting wet."],"teach1":["Focus on give your listener a clear character and place.","Hold this teaching point: An orientation tells listeners who is involved and where events occur. Without it, later pronouns and actions are confus","Worked comparison: One option: \"At the school oval, Ben ran towards the gate to catch his rolling ball.\" It answers who, where and why."],"teach2":["Focus on choose the most useful notes.","Hold this teaching point: Speaking notes should be short prompts, not every word of the speech. Key nouns and verbs help you remember without read","Worked comparison: \"gardener / bucket / beside tree\" preserves the important detail; full grammar is not required in notes."],"teach3":["Focus on speak, listen and improve one detail.","Hold this teaching point: A practice retell lets you check if events make sense aloud. Use pauses and an audible voice; invite a listener to say w","Worked comparison: \"Rain soaked the path. My sister opened her umbrella.\" Pause naturally after the first complete thought."],"write":["Use the independent instructions, not the model words.","Give an original response and the reason behind it.","Independent reminder: Plan a real or invented four-event story about an unexpected visitor. Make four brief cue lines (not full sentences), tell the story in four or five complete sp"]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use orientation with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify event and point to the words or events that support it.","I can practise cue words independently on a new prompt.","I can notice listener needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying orientation independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to tell events in an order that makes sense?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nI tell a short story about losing my hat: First I put it on near the oval. Next the wind carried it over the fence. Then the groundskeeper returned it. Finally I thanked her. Each event leads naturally to the next.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nPut these events in sensible order: We dried the dog; the dog jumped in a puddle; we went home.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nThe dog jumped in a puddle. We went home. We dried the dog. Drying follows getting wet.","explain":"A listener needs to know what happened first, next and at the end. Temporal words signal changes, but events must also connect logically.\n\nA listener needs to know what happened first, next and at the end. Temporal words signal changes, but events must also connect logically. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nDo not assume saying finally makes an event a logical ending. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Tell events in an order that makes sense","visual":[{"alt":"Story events ordered as hat on oval then wind carrying it over the fence.","caption":"Events follow a cause: the hat must be worn before the wind can take it.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">First</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">hat on near the oval</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Next</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">wind carries it over the fence</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying event independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to give your listener a clear character and place?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nInstead of starting \"It went missing\", I begin \"On Saturday, Zara left her red kite beside the beach path.\" Now a listener can picture Zara, the kite and the location.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nImprove the opening \"He ran away\" by naming the character, place and reason for moving.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\nOne option: \"At the school oval, Ben ran towards the gate to catch his rolling ball.\" It answers who, where and why.","explain":"An orientation tells listeners who is involved and where events occur. Without it, later pronouns and actions are confusing.\n\nAn orientation tells listeners who is involved and where events occur. Without it, later pronouns and actions are confusing. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nA listener cannot see the picture in your head; introduce new people and places. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Give your listener a clear character and place","visual":[{"alt":"Brief cue notes oval and hat beside the full spoken sentence they prompt.","caption":"Notes stay short so the speaker tells the story instead of reading a script.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cue notes</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">oval — hat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Spoken sentence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">I put my hat on near the oval.</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying cue words independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to choose the most useful notes?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nFor my hat story, I write four lines: oval—hat; wind—fence; groundskeeper—returns; thank you. I can tell the complete sentences aloud from these cues.\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nShorten \"Then the gardener found the missing bucket beside the tree\" into three or four note words.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\n\"gardener / bucket / beside tree\" preserves the important detail; full grammar is not required in notes.","explain":"Speaking notes should be short prompts, not every word of the speech. Key nouns and verbs help you remember without reading a script.\n\nSpeaking notes should be short prompts, not every word of the speech. Key nouns and verbs help you remember without reading a script. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nDo not copy whole paragraphs into note cards, because you may read rather than tell. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Choose the most useful notes"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The best approach uses the specific concept, evidence and reasoning before practising independently; copying or inventing unsupported facts does not show understanding.","options":["Use the example and explain the clue or language choice before trying listener independently.","Copy every sentence exactly without explaining it.","Add a made-up event and describe it as a fact.","Ignore the task and choose an unrelated detail."],"question":"Which is the strongest next step when learning to speak, listen and improve one detail?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WATCH MY THINKING\nI tell the hat story, pause after the wind blows, and ask \"Could you tell where the hat went?\" If the listener asks which fence, I add \"the fence beside the tennis court\".\n\nYOUR SMALL TRY\nRead these notes aloud as a two-sentence story: rain—path / sister—umbrella. Add a pause between events.\n\nCOMPARE AND CORRECT\n\"Rain soaked the path. My sister opened her umbrella.\" Pause naturally after the first complete thought.","explain":"A practice retell lets you check if events make sense aloud. Use pauses and an audible voice; invite a listener to say which part was unclear.\n\nA practice retell lets you check if events make sense aloud. Use pauses and an audible voice; invite a listener to say which part was unclear. This matters because a reader or listener can only understand your ideas when you notice how the language works, test your understanding on specific examples and explain the evidence for your choice. Keep the meaning of the original text rather than changing facts to make an answer sound impressive. Read the model closely: which particular word, event or detail changes the meaning? Try explaining the same decision to someone else without repeating the model sentence. Then compare your new attempt with the feedback and revise anything that does not fit the actual evidence.","icon":"📖","notice":"WATCH OUT\nSpeaking quickly does not make a story clear; rehearse meaning and pace. Re-read the prompt and correct the misunderstanding before moving on.","title":"Speak, listen and improve one detail"}],"title":"Year 3: Tell a story and prepare readable notes","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"Narrative: story elements and planning","week":1,"week_in_unit":1,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"I DO — I tell a short story about losing my hat: First I put it on near the oval. Next the wind carried it over the fence. Then the groundskeeper returned it. Finally I thanked her. Each event leads naturally to the next.\n\nNow look for the exact evidence in the words or actions. Instead of starting \"It went missing\", I begin \"On Saturday, Zara left her red kite beside the beach path.\" Now a listener can picture Zara, the kite and the location.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"Story events ordered as hat on oval then wind carrying it over the fence.","caption":"Events follow a cause: the hat must be worn before the wind can take it.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">First</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">hat on near the oval</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Next</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">wind carries it over the fence</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Brief cue notes oval and hat beside the full spoken sentence they prompt.","caption":"Notes stay short so the speaker tells the story instead of reading a script.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Cue notes</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">oval — hat</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Spoken sentence</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">I put my hat on near the oval.</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 3"}') WHERE seed_key='s2-eng-y3-w01-l4' AND family_id IS NULL;
